Installation
============

winless is one C program (C11 + POSIX: sockets, poll) plus the viewer page viewer.html. It needs a C compiler and make.
To open a window under WSL it also needs a Chromium-based browser on the Windows side (Microsoft Edge is preinstalled on
Windows 10/11); without one, open the printed http://localhost:PORT/ address in any browser.

    ./configure [--prefix=/usr/local]
    make
    make check            # builds and runs the C tests in tests/ (no extra tools needed)
    make check-asan       # the same tests against an AddressSanitizer/UBSan build
    make install          # honours DESTDIR
    make uninstall
    make dist             # winless-VERSION.tar.gz

Run it in place without installing:  ./winless open FILE   (it finds viewer.html next to the executable).

Building from a git checkout needs autoconf 2.69 or later to regenerate ./configure from configure.ac (`autoconf`);
release tarballs ship a generated configure.  Options for configure are the usual ones (--prefix, --bindir, --datadir,
--mandir, CC=, CFLAGS=).

Using winless on remote hosts (sshw)
------------------------------------

`make install` also installs `sshw` (a link to winless), its manual page, and the sources as winless-src.tar.gz beside viewer.html.  If libc.a and
libsqlite3.a are installed (libc6-dev, libsqlite3-dev), `make` also builds a static winless for this platform into remote/OS-MACHINE/winless, which `sshw`
uploads to hosts that have none.  Without them everything else works; sshw then needs winless installed on the hosts, or a compiler there to build it.
Requires OpenSSH 6.7 or later on this machine.  See sshw(1).
